mqvpn (0.16.3+20261007-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=high

  * fix(server): a fixed-IP user reconnecting before its old tunnel timed
    out (router reboot, lost close on a dead WAN) mapped two live
    connections to one pool slot: n_sessions leaked one client slot per
    overlap until max_clients was reached and every new tunnel got 403
    "max clients reached" until the server restarted (GHSA-vv9v-hp7m-mmhc).
    The newest connection now supersedes the old one through
    svr_session_release and the old connection is closed; the server logs
    "fixed IP reused by a new connection, superseding the old one". Two
    routers sharing one fixed-IP user now take turns instead of silently
    corrupting the session table. Multipath clients are unaffected: all
    WANs of one client are paths of a single connection. New e2e
    run_pinned_ip_double_test.sh.
  * fix(server): a client authenticated with the shared [Auth] Key could
    name itself after a configured user through the x-user header and take
    that user's fixed IP; the name was also not checked for the characters
    add_user rejects, so it could break the control-API JSON
    (GHSA-vv9v-hp7m-mmhc). Such an x-user now falls back to "(global)" and
    the server logs "x-user rejected". Per-user-key setups (the OMR
    default) were not affected. New e2e run_xuser_spoof_test.sh.
  * fix(client): use-after-free when the CONNECT-IP tunnel stream closed
    while the connection stayed up (server RESET_STREAM or an invalid
    ROUTE_ADVERTISEMENT): the next path activation or a set_path_weight /
    set_path_dscp_mask control call sent a path label through the freed
    request (GHSA-jgf6-pf7m-gr65). The pointer is now cleared on close.
  * All three were reported by Pierre-Yves Le Borgne and are fork-only:
    upstream mqvpn has no fixed IPs, no x-user and no path-label sync. No
    wire or ABI change; old and new clients and servers interoperate.

 -- mqvpn maintainers <maintainers@example.com>  Wed, 07 Oct 2026 17:06:06 +0200

mqvpn (0.16.3-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * feat(client): PinnedPubkey (INI [Server] PinnedPubkey, JSON
    pinned_pubkey, --pinned-pubkey, mqvpn_config_set_pinned_pubkey()) --
    pin the server's public key (base64 SHA-256 of its SubjectPublicKeyInfo,
    curl --pinnedpubkey format, "sha256//" optional, up to 4 ';'-separated
    for rotation). Insecure verifies nothing, so any on-path server that
    completes the handshake received the PSK in the CONNECT-IP Authorization
    header and could replay it (GHSA-qq6x-5r9f-2w3m). With a pin, the
    client checks the leaf key (proven by the TLS 1.3 CertificateVerify)
    right after the handshake and closes before sending anything; the pin
    replaces CA, hostname and expiry validation, so a self-signed server
    needs no Insecure, and it overrides Insecure when both are set. A
    malformed pin is a startup error. New e2e run_pinned_pubkey_test.sh
    reproduces the leak with Insecure and checks the pin blocks it.
  * fix(client): reconnect over a live path when the initial path is
    removed -- removing the path that carries path_id 0 closes the
    connection, and tick_reconnect() counts the detached path as a backup,
    so with two WANs only one primary was left and
    mqvpn_rotate_primary_path() returned the removed path: every reconnect
    retried it and the tunnel stayed down until that WAN came back
    (omr-tracker's 005-mqvpn-path removes a failed WAN's path this way).
    The rotation now moves off a current path that is a backup, and the
    new mqvpn_reconnect_path_idx() falls back to the first attached
    BackupPath when no attached primary is left, so Path + BackupPath
    also reconnects over the backup. New e2e run_primary_removal_test.sh
    (Pierre-Yves Le Borgne, PR #1).
  * Merge upstream mqvpn main (0.16.3):
  * refactor: ABI 3 transport contract -- the library core no longer
    touches sockets. mqvpn_client_add_path() now takes a per-path transport
    (ops table + opaque ctx) instead of an fd, mqvpn_client_add_path_fd(),
    mqvpn_path_desc_t.fd and the send_packet API are gone, and
    mqvpn_client_on_platform_fd_closed() becomes
    mqvpn_client_on_platform_path_released(). The bundled POSIX bind
    (src/bind/, also used by the server with per-connection TX scopes) and
    a Winsock bind carry the UDP I/O, GSO/GRO included. path_mgr is
    removed: Linux, Darwin and Windows keep per-path state in one slot
    table, which also fixes two re-add bugs (a slot that still owns its
    socket is reactivated instead of re-added; a path whose library slot
    was recycled for another path is re-added again).
  * refactor(platform): shared POSIX network-monitor decision layer
    (netmon_common.c) for Linux netlink and Darwin route monitoring, and a
    shared CLI-to-library config bridge (client_config_bridge.c) so a
    setting can no longer be forwarded on one platform and dropped on
    another (InitMaxPathId was, on Windows).
  * feat(config): mqvpn_config_set_cert_verifier() -- platform-owned TLS
    verification (xquic APP_VERIFY); the client reports MQVPN_ERR_TLS only
    for a verifier rejection and warns when Insecure overrides a verifier.
    Windows verifies against the CryptoAPI stores unless SSL_CERT_FILE /
    SSL_CERT_DIR is set; Android and iOS install platform verifiers
    (PlatformTrust / SecTrust). The server certificate file must be the
    full chain: clients do not fetch missing intermediates.
  * fix(server): send IPv6 ADDRESS_ASSIGN as /128 (RFC 9484 S4.7.1); the
    pool prefix with host bits set advertised the whole pool as permitted
    sources. The client widens its TUN width locally (/112 cap), so it
    still works with older servers.
  * fix(platform): exit non-zero when tunnel setup aborts (TUN, address,
    MTU, routes or kill switch failure), so Restart=on-failure acts
    instead of leaving a process with no tunnel and no retry; break the
    event loop on SIGTERM/SIGINT when the client is already CLOSED (only
    SIGKILL stopped it before).
  * fix(routing): read `ip route get` to EOF -- musl iproute2 (OpenWrt,
    Alpine) writes its two output lines separately, so a single read()
    could miss the second, SIGPIPE `ip` and abort the tunnel with "could
    not determine original iface"; roll back ::/1 when the 8000::/1
    catch-all fails (it was left routed into the TUN after disconnect).
  * fix(hybrid): cap the stream backlog waiting unsent in xquic so it no
    longer delays DATAGRAMs; budget the server egress relay loop so
    parallel TCP flows share the send grant; destroy an egress flow before
    resetting its stream (an EPIPE loop logged ~64k lines in two seconds).
  * fix(status): read the whole control-API reply -- `mqvpn --status`
    truncated get_status at 32 KiB (about 15 clients) and printed a short,
    valid-looking client list.
  * fix(windows): remove the WFP kill switch objects through a dynamic
    session; the old cleanup left every filter, block-all included, in
    place until reboot.
  * fix: keep the Linux netlink message cursor signed; Darwin destroys the
    client before freeing callback-owned platform objects.
  * chore(deps): bump the xquic fork to upstream mqvpn-main -- WLB
    scheduler series (a silently blackholed path no longer stalls the
    weight rounds: 40/100 -> 1/100 failures over 100 blackhole cycles),
    two-tier stream reassembly cap, framed-but-unsent stream cap,
    APP_VERIFY, BoringSSL 0.20260903.0.
  * android/ios: engine on a dedicated reactor thread with paths through
    the bundled bind; Android keeps Wi-Fi, cellular and Ethernet up
    together (optional Bluetooth path); iOS tunnel session coordinator.
  * Merge port of dev features onto ABI 3: backup paths and the
    control-API add_path/remove_path/list_paths/set_path_weight/
    set_path_dscp_mask commands run on the Linux slot table (a path
    removed through the API is never re-added by the monitor); re-added
    paths keep their backup flag, weight and dscp_mask; dev's client
    settings (auth username, TLS ciphers, FEC, path-label sync/push,
    WRTT/WRR/redundant/DSCP schedulers) go through the shared config
    bridge on every platform; removing the initial path still sends the
    closing frames over a live sibling; `ip` is searched in PATH before
    the absolute-path fallbacks.
  * build: bump the libmqvpn SOVERSION to 4 (libmqvpn.so.4). The ABI 3
    transport contract removed mqvpn_client_add_path_fd(),
    mqvpn_client_add_path_fd_with_outcome(),
    mqvpn_client_on_platform_fd_closed() and mqvpn_server_set_socket_fd()
    from the library, which still carried soname 3: programs linked
    against 0.16.0 to 0.16.2 must be rebuilt against the new header.
    Refresh libmqvpn0.symbols for libmqvpn.so.4 and ship
    mqvpn_bind_posix.h in libmqvpn-dev.

 -- mqvpn maintainers <maintainers@example.com>  Mon, 05 Oct 2026 18:00:00 +0200

mqvpn (0.16.2-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * fix(server): one live CONNECT-IP tunnel per connection, stream-scoped
    session lifetime -- a second Extended CONNECT connect-ip on the same H3
    connection overwrote conn->assigned_ip and left the old sessions[]
    entry dangling: heap use-after-free on later TUN routing or server
    destroy, plus an addr-pool leak and n_sessions drift, reachable by any
    authenticated client. Reject a duplicate with 409 while the tunnel
    stream is live; release the whole session when the tunnel stream
    closes (RFC 9484 S4.1) and allow re-establishment on a fresh stream;
    drop datagrams not associated with the current tunnel stream (RFC 9297
    S2.1); give each tunnel generation fresh reorder engines, with reorder
    negotiation now scoped to the establishing request; addr_pool rejects
    wrapping bases so 0.0.0.0 can never be assigned.
  * fix(client): mqvpn_client_connect() called manually from RECONNECTING
    now runs the same pre-start path-slot reset as the internal retry
    timer, and the in_connect re-entrancy fence is released right after
    cli_start_connection() commits (not left held across the callbacks it
    fires) so a callback cancelling the retry via disconnect() keeps
    working.
  * fix: four small correctness fixes -- client no longer counts
    dgram_sent on -XQC_EAGAIN (xquic wrote no frame); set_server_addr /
    set_socket_fd reject an oversized addrlen instead of silently
    clamping it; recovery_rollback now closes the fd before notifying the
    platform layer on linux/darwin/windows so the CLOSED_DROPPED ->
    CLOSED_FREE gate can fire; reorder_rx's tick skips its two 1024-bucket
    sweeps when there are no flows.
  * fix(windows): route Ctrl+C through the loop thread instead of calling
    mqvpn_client_disconnect() from the OS signal thread (single-thread
    contract violation); free platform objects (TUN/events/path fds) after
    library teardown instead of before, matching the Linux shutdown order;
    forward InitMaxPathId / --init-max-path-id to the client config, which
    was silently dropped on Windows only.
  * fix(control-api): get_stats/get_status/get_build_info/get_fec_stats/
    get_all_fec_stats/get_reorder_stats were silently server-only -- every
    field read all-zero counters, "unknown" scheduler, or "fec not built"
    on a client-mode router regardless of real traffic, because the
    handlers unconditionally read the (NULL in client mode) server handle;
    found live on an OpenMPTCProuter client-mode bench mid-tunnel. Add
    client-mode counterparts (mqvpn_client_get_stats/get_info/
    scheduler_label/uptime_seconds/get_fec_stats) and dispatch on the
    control socket's client context; documented in docs/control-api.md.
  * chore(server): add debug-build invariant checks for the session table
    (svr_check_session_invariants(), a no-op under NDEBUG) -- asserts every
    non-NULL sessions[] slot maps back to its own offset, that the
    non-NULL count equals n_sessions, and that n_sessions stays within
    [0, max_clients]; checked after establishment, after
    svr_session_release(), and before the destroy sweep.
  * chore(deps): bump the xquic fork to cd00481 -- the stream reassembly
    cap (CWE-770) is now non-fatal: an over-cap STREAM frame is dropped
    unacked instead of escalating into a FRAME_ENCODING_ERROR connection
    close, which was killing healthy multipath transfers under cross-path
    reordering; one slot stays reserved for prefix-extending frames so the
    hole remains fillable, FIN completion is re-checked on the duplicate
    path, and the cap is tunable via max_stream_frame_buffered_cnt
    (0 = default 8192). Also bumps BoringSSL to 0.20260813.0.
  * bench(rtmp): add an RTMP-over-mqvpn bonding benchmark suite -- tier-1
    netem matrix and tier-2 real-footage runs comparing a direct RTMP
    publish against one bonded through mqvpn across link flaps, with lag
    charts and side-by-side comparison videos; report published to the
    website and README.
  * test: double CONNECT-IP regression coverage for the session-lifetime
    fix; manual-reconnect-from-RECONNECTING regression coverage.

